WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.200
This video was made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.310
Learn new skills that you can use at home,
work, or school with a free trial of Skillshare

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.919
Premium at the link in the description.

00:00:09.919 --> 00:00:15.250
Normal times in business are like a road.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:19.740
Competitors are all heading in the same direction
and, while some might turn away and try a

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:25.540
different path, for the most part, the only
way to get ahead is through brute force—to

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.390
slowly catch up and take the lead.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.130
That is difficult.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:35.690
Abnormal times in business, though, when an
industry is in crisis, are like a fork in

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:36.690
the road.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:41.220
The competitors all reach it together and
are forced to pick a path.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.820
This levels the playing field.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.060
While the big players have the resources to
evaluate the different paths, and might have

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:52.300
experience from times when this has happened
before, all companies big and small must pick

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.820
a path, and at the end of the day, only one
is going to work best.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.379
Crisis can be an equalizer.

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:05.379
It stress-tests the big incumbent firms to
see if they’re still the strongest, or riding

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:09.979
on inertia, and gives smaller players the
opportunity to make bold choices that could

00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:12.049
put them ahead.

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:15.460
This is what’s happening in the airline
industry right now.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.409
It’s one of the clusters of companies that,
as a result of the Coronavirus pandemic, had

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:25.909
to almost entirely shut down, and is now in
the process of turning back on and reinventing

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:31.340
itself to capture a new set of consumers with
new needs and wants.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.810
Industry turmoil is happening, and that means
that there’s a case to be made that we’re

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.090
at that fork in the road—that all airlines
are having to pick a path, and that this could

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.429
lead to a shuffling of the rank and order
of the industry.

00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:48.759
There’s a case to be made that right now,
for the first time in a long time, there’s

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:55.859
real opportunity for something new—there’s
opportunity for airline entrepreneurship.

00:01:55.859 --> 00:02:01.770
If you were tasked with starting an accounting
business, or a coffee shop, or a landscaping

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.899
business, you’d probably know roughly what
you needed to do to start—you’d need to

00:02:05.899 --> 00:02:11.760
get a license, or a retail space, or some
lawnmowers—and, with enough initiative and

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.950
a little bit of luck, you’d probably even
have a real shot at successfully starting

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.490
any one of these.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:22.640
Meanwhile, if you were tasked with starting
an airline, you and I both know that, no matter

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.420
your luck or initiative, you’d have almost
no shot of ever taking off.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:33.549
Due to its complexity and capital intensity,
successful airline entrepreneurship is one

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:39.321
of the most difficult endeavors in business,
but it is something that, occasionally, real

00:02:39.321 --> 00:02:42.239
people actually do.

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:46.510
So what does it take—what is step one of
forming a new airline?

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.519
Well, first, an airline needs a business model.

00:02:50.519 --> 00:02:55.310
Now, everything for start-up airlines nowadays
builds off of what makes them unique, and

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.850
new airlines have to be unique.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.910
You see, fundamentally, for a new company
to succeed, they need to do something better

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:08.140
than their competitors—they need to be cheaper,
faster, more convenient, or just do something

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.189
that others do not.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:13.470
If they don’t, then why would customers
chose to switch to them?

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:18.450
The competitive advantage of major airlines
is that they are major—it’s that you can

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:23.370
get on one of their planes on one side of
the world and, after a few connections, get

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:24.940
off on the other.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:29.549
The problem is that almost all of the major
full-service network carriers, as those in

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:33.609
the industry will call them, were formed in
the early days of commercial aviation, and

00:03:33.609 --> 00:03:35.099
grew as the industry did.

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:40.180
Today, it would just be effectively impossible
for an airline to launch with enough scale

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:45.430
to compete directly with United, British Airways,
or Singapore Airlines, and if they didn’t,

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:49.519
the legacy airlines would crush them faster
than they could get to the necessary scale

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:52.900
for their competitive advantage to be considered
their size.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:58.019
Of the ten largest airlines in the US, none
were founded in the current century, and in

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:04.269
Europe, only one was—Wizz Air, which nobody
would consider a full-service network carrier.

00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:09.459
In fact, globally, one of the only examples
of a full-service network carrier established

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:14.170
within the past few decades without government
support is Virgin Australia, and in their

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.920
case, it was almost by accident.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:21.530
The collapse of Ansett Australia, which was
the country’s only major full-service network

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:25.610
carrier aside from Qantas at the time, left
a massive void in Australia’s aviation market

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:30.250
only a year into Virgin Australia's operations,
which gave the company the opportunity to

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:32.760
grow quite quickly and effectively.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:38.050
However, even this example gives further credence
to the theory that it’s nearly impossible

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:43.800
for a new major full-service network carrier
to emerge in the 21st century as Virgin Australia

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:49.920
entered administration in 2020, and is now
exiting as a smaller, primarily domestic airline—ceding

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.130
the international market to its legacy competitor,
Qantas.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:05:00.540
So, once again, new airlines need a strong,
defined, and unique business model to succeed.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:05.650
Looking at three of the most promising startup
airlines planned to launch in 2021, a strong,

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:10.560
defined, and unique business model is exactly
what each of them has.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.530
FlyPop, for example, is planning to launch
long-haul, low-cost flights from the UK to

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:20.540
secondary cities in India without existing
non-stop service, such as Amritsar and Ahmedabad,

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:24.150
in order to cater to the Indian diaspora living
in the UK.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:28.560
Meanwhile, Play Airlines is looking to reprise
the business model of the now-defunct WowAir,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.690
and was even founded by former Wow executives,
by focusing on facilitating low-cost transatlantic

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.370
travel with connecting itineraries through
Iceland.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:40.700
Then, in what is likely the most promising
airline start-up of 2021—as its founder

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:45.220
previously launched WestJet, Azul Airways,
and JetBlue—Breeze Airways is looking to

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:50.100
launch what they refer to as “hub-busting”
flights on underserved routes in the US—meaning

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:54.720
they’re identifying routes without existing
non-stop service such as Tulsa to New York

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.640
or Louisville to Los Angeles, and capturing
demand by setting up non-stop service.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:05.220
Crucially, each of these three business models
do not involve directly competing with full-service

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.750
network carriers.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:11.570
Budget airlines like Play Airlines and FlyPop
tend to expand the pie by offering prices

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:15.950
that stimulate demand from a lower market
segment that likely would not regularly fly

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:20.390
on a full-service network carrier, while Breeze
Airways will likely most directly compete

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:25.550
with Southwest—as it’s the primary incumbent
airline that might offer non-stop, hub-busting

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:26.600
flights.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:31.550
In the selection of a business model, not
competing against the world’s largest airlines

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:36.660
is a good choice, because full-service network
carriers have the time and money to start

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:40.880
and win a price war, while start-up airlines
do not.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:46.090
That’s because it’s very difficult for
start-up airlines to get funding.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:51.540
There just isn’t the appetite for it, because
the the airline industry has very little upside

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:52.570
for investors.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:57.870
It goes through massive, dramatic cycles of
profit and loss depending on oil prices and

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:03.770
external economic conditions, but even in
the best of times, it makes a very thin profit.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:09.740
Over the past 25 years, even before the Coronavirus
pandemic, the industry has hovered around

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:12.740
a four to five percent operating margin.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:17.040
Investors could just as easily put their money
into something more secure—like the railroad

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.080
industry, which hovers between a 25 and 30
percent operating margin.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:25.750
In fact, that’s exactly what investor Warren
Buffet has done—he’s famously shirked

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:30.470
significant investments in airlines, while
taking massive, successful bets on the American

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:31.550
railroad industry.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:36.370
For this reason, many start-up airlines fail
before launch simply because they cannot secure

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:41.710
funding, or after launch because they didn't
secure enough to reach critical mass.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:47.250
For those three most promising start-up airlines
of 2021, though, they each have seemingly

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.700
found enough money to launch.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:55.130
Play Airlines secured a $42 million investment
from Avianta Capital, which is an Irish investment

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:59.680
fund run by Aislinn Whittley-Ryan—a member
of an influential aviation family which includes

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.270
the founders of Ryanair.

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:06.660
Meanwhile, FlyPop's exact investor makeup
is less clear, but their largest source of

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.120
funding is coming from a UK government fund
intended to kick-start innovative businesses

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.240
whose launch was affected by COVID.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:18.530
Finally, Breeze took the most traditional
funding path, having raised $83.3 million

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:23.020
through the venture capital industry, but
that was likely only possible due to its founder’s

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:27.200
remarkably consistent track record of successful
airline startups.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:33.000
The only consistency with start-up airline
funding is inconsistency—unlike other industries,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.640
where there is often a defined ecosystem of
investors willing and sometimes competing

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:42.560
to fund startups with a strong business model,
plan, executive team, and proof of concept,

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.470
you just don’t have that with airlines.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:50.400
The tiny number of new entrants plus the general
lack of appetite from investors means that

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.350
finding funding to complete the extraordinarily
expensive tasks required to set up an airline

00:08:55.350 --> 00:09:00.370
is itself the task that prevents most from
reaching takeoff.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:05.410
One of those extraordinarily expensive tasks
involves quite simply gaining the legal right

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:07.310
to operate an airline.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:12.200
Every national aviation authority issues what
are typically called air operator’s certificates,

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.930
which allows a company to use their aircraft
for commercial purposes.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:21.020
In the US, there are essentially two choices
of certificate type for scheduled airlines.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:26.380
The first, and simpler one, is a part 135
certificate—referring to the section of

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:31.050
the US’ federal aviation regulations that
outline this certificate’s operating requirements.

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:37.560
Now, a part 135 certificate is primarily used
for cargo and passenger charter operations—which

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.150
often includes private jet operations—but
it can also be used for certain scheduled

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:43.150
operations.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:47.610
Specifically, this certificate only allows
the use of aircraft with nine passenger seats

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:51.350
or fewer on scheduled operations, with a few
exceptions.

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:56.570
One of the largest part 135 scheduled airlines
in the US, for example, is Cape Air, which

00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:01.700
operates a fleet of 94 nine-seat aircraft
to primarily small, government subsidized

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:04.850
destinations around the US and Caribbean.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:09.200
Because they are on a part 135 certificate,
they can do things like operate flights with

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:14.030
a single pilot—even though in practice most
of their flights operate with two pilots—whereas

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:19.170
a part 121 certificate holder, like United
Airlines, for example, would need two pilots

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:23.760
even to operate the exact same aircraft on
the exact same route.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:29.250
In general, the regulations for part 135 operations
are slightly less stringent, and that also

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.700
means that attaining this license is a lot
easier.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:38.300
Costs apparently average between $35,000 and
$95,000 in legal fees, plus an additional

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:42.920
$50,000 in expenses to attain a part 135 certificate.

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:49.750
Meanwhile, getting a part 121 air operator’s
certificate is much, much more difficult.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:54.710
These are the certificates held by United,
American Airlines, Delta, and any other large,

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:59.300
commercial airline in the US, and the process
to attain one is so complicated that it’s

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:04.040
essentially impossible without hiring a third-party
consultancy to assist.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:10.490
This, in and of itself, is such as niche process
that there are only six FAA-endorsed part

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:14.270
121 certification consultancies in existence.

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:20.610
After all, there are only 104 active part
121 airlines in the US, so a new certification

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.410
is a fairly rare occurrence.

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:28.310
While none of the FAA-endorsed certification
consultancies publicize prices, a non-endorsed

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:33.540
competitor lists their consulting fee as $1.2
million—which includes things like creating

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:37.610
an operations manual, training management,
setting up electronic flight bags, establishing

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:41.930
a drug and alcohol testing program, and almost
everything else needed prior to application,

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.320
plus the management of the process of actually
applying itself.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:50.660
In general, the reason this application process
is so strenuous is because a start-up airline

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:54.920
must set up the internal infrastructure for
all the extensive safety programs needed to

00:11:54.920 --> 00:12:00.300
pass the FAA’s review, and then it must
present a massive packet of documents proving

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.150
that it has done all these things.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:08.510
Given the complexity of this process, some
airlines decide to purchase, rather than apply

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.960
for, an air operator's certificate.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:16.690
This is what Breeze Airways initially planned
to do—in July, they applied for the US Department

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:21.040
of Transportation to transfer the certificate
of now-defunct Compass Airlines to them—but

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:26.030
they eventually dropped this plan due to push-back
from other airlines and Compass’ pilot’s

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:27.030
union.

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:30.300
Now, Breeze is going through the process of
applying for its own air operator’s certificate,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:35.670
but it goes to show that buying an existing
one is a very real possibility, and it’s

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.920
something that happens fairly frequently with
part 135 certificates.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:45.100
In fact, there are even websites listing small
part 135 airlines for sale—which can be

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:48.130
bought specifically for their certificates.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:52.630
Around the world, while each country’s exact
process is different, certification is quite

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:57.870
often a major hurdle for a new airline as
each country’s air regulator needs to see

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:02.730
that the startup is fit-to-fly before giving
them their stamp of approval.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:07.470
Of course, these previous steps, of deciding
on a business model, finding funding, and

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:11.630
attaining an air operator’s certificate,
have nothing to do with the most fundamental

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.980
thing an airline needs to operate—airplanes.

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:21.700
Now, somewhat counterintuitively, acquiring
airplanes is actually one of the simpler phases

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:24.100
of the airline start-up process.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:30.790
The reason why this is is because, quite often,
airlines don’t actually own aircraft themselves.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:35.670
This is part of the reason why Breeze, for
example, can launch with only about $90 million

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:40.690
in funding, despite the fact that a single
one of their planned flagship aircraft, the

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:44.680
A220-300, has a list price of about $90 million.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:50.540
Rather, a startup airline like Breeze tends
to use one of two techniques.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:52.899
The first option is to lease.

00:13:52.899 --> 00:13:57.220
This works essentially like a car lease, just
on a much larger scale.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:14:02.400
Some 50% of commercial aircraft globally are
under lease, and this option is often more

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.740
popular with smaller airlines.

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:09.591
The advantages of leasing aircraft are straightforward—it
spreads out the cost, without the need to

00:14:09.591 --> 00:14:13.779
take on debt, and it reduces risk, as lease
terms can be shorter than the useful life

00:14:13.779 --> 00:14:17.700
of an aircraft, are there can be some options
to return aircraft before the end of their

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.900
lease if there’s no longer the need.

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:25.649
In addition, leasing aircraft allows airlines
to get aircraft faster.

00:14:25.649 --> 00:14:29.510
Aircraft manufacturers often have a multi-year
backlog.

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:35.810
Airbus, for example, as of December, 2020,
has a 7,000 aircraft order backlog, meaning

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:40.740
an airline ordering an aircraft today wouldn’t
receive it for many years.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:45.540
Lessors often have aircraft available within
months or even weeks, making this the attractive

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.310
option for startups.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:53.480
Also, particularly nowadays, COVID has made
getting an airplane on lease easier.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:57.770
Existing airlines have been downsizing their
fleets and getting rid of older, less fuel-efficient

00:14:57.770 --> 00:14:58.770
aircraft.

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:04.170
In the case of American Airlines and Air Canada,
that meant retiring 20 and 14, respectively,

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:06.120
of their E190 aircraft.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:11.750
It so happens that Breeze is launching with
E190 aircraft and, even though they aren’t

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:16.420
directly leasing the retired aircraft of American
and Air Canada, more aircraft of a type in

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:19.150
the marketplace pushes prices down.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:24.399
There is, however, another mechanism increasingly
used by airlines, especially when they need

00:15:24.399 --> 00:15:28.600
to get newer aircraft for which there isn’t
a large second-hand market.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:34.190
Breeze, for example, has an order with Airbus
for sixty A220-300 aircraft.

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:40.290
Now, this order is directly from Breeze to
Airbus, but Breeze themselves could never

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:42.820
pay for sixty of these aircraft.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:46.940
Therefore, what they’ve done is, before
they’ve even taken delivery of the first

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:51.790
of these aircraft, sell five of them to another
company, Voyager Aviation Holdings, which

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:55.230
is then going to lease these aircraft back
to Breeze.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:16:00.070
This means Breeze is effectively getting the
exact aircraft it wants, when it wants, but

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:02.860
functionally they get all the benefits of
a lease.

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:07.690
It’s to be expected that, as the delivery
dates of the rest of the airline’s A220

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:12.690
fleet near, the airline will enter into more
of these leaseback agreements.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:17.420
Of course this is all a simplistic view of
the process of airline entrepreneurship, but

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:23.290
business model selection, funding, certification,
and aircraft acquisition are the four largest

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:29.610
hurdles—and improper completion of any of
these is often the source of a start-up airline’s

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:30.850
eventual failure.

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:35.350
The rest of the process, from hiring staff,
finding office-space, crafting a marketing

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:40.839
strategy, leasing space at airports, and much,
much more, is much less likely to have a dramatic

00:16:40.839 --> 00:16:43.670
impact on their odds of success.

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:48.270
Since the era of the emergence of low-cost
airlines, there’s been little successful

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:50.850
action in airline entrepreneurship.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:55.360
With a business atmosphere primed for disruption,
the next few years will prove whether it’s

00:16:55.360 --> 00:17:00.460
still possible for new airlines to succeed,
or whether the industry is still locked down

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:03.709
by the world’s largest airlines.

00:17:03.709 --> 00:17:08.939
What this video goes to prove is that, even
for the world’s toughest tasks, like starting

00:17:08.939 --> 00:17:12.919
an airline, there’s someone out there doing
it successfully.

00:17:12.919 --> 00:17:18.539
That’s a lot of what the philosophy is Skillshare
is based on—they figure that there’s nobody

00:17:18.539 --> 00:17:22.649
better to teach skills than the people who
actually use those skills.

00:17:22.649 --> 00:17:26.999
Their classes, of which there are thousands,
are taught by real people who actually know

00:17:26.999 --> 00:17:31.039
what they’re talking about, so it’s a
great way to learn for anyone who’s creative

00:17:31.039 --> 00:17:35.149
and curious—which I’m sure includes all
Wendover viewers.

00:17:35.149 --> 00:17:39.429
Whether you want to get ahead at work or find
a new passion, Skillshare certainly has a

00:17:39.429 --> 00:17:40.769
class for you.

00:17:40.769 --> 00:17:44.679
For example, if you want to start out as a
YouTuber, you can take the Creative Video

00:17:44.679 --> 00:17:49.110
Storytelling &amp; Editing: Making the Most of
Stock Footage class by Nikki Stephens, which

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:53.409
is a lot of what us educational creators do—we
can’t go to what we’re talking about most

00:17:53.409 --> 00:17:57.539
of the time, and we often can’t afford to
animate our whole videos, so we rely on using

00:17:57.539 --> 00:18:00.279
existing stock footage creatively.

00:18:00.279 --> 00:18:04.519
Skillshare is built to be the best place to
learn, meaning there are no ads and they’re

00:18:04.519 --> 00:18:09.461
always launching new classes, so if you have
any curiosity at all in gaining a new skill

00:18:09.461 --> 00:18:13.840
of any type, check them out with a free trial
of Skillshare Premium, which is available

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:17.289
to the first 1,000 people to use the link
in the description, and you’ll be helping

00:18:17.289 --> 00:18:19.639
support this channel while you’re at it.

